**What the MAP-splitting rule is**

For every patient we build "cover periods" from their pharmacy and medical claims. Each drug gets its own periods: the days we believe the patient had that drug, based on fills and days of supply. We call these MAP periods.

A line of therapy is the group of drugs a patient is on together. The line keeps going as long as the same drugs continue. When a **new** drug is added, the current line ends and a new line starts.

The splitting rule is about one specific case: **a drug from an earlier line comes back later.**

Example:

- Line 1: drug A + drug B. The patient stops both.
- Drug C starts. That is a new drug, so Line 2 starts. Fine so far.
- Months later, while still on drug C, **drug B comes back**.

Today the engine treats the returning drug B like any other added drug. B was not part of Line 2, so B is "new" to this line — and the line splits: Line 2 ends, Line 3 starts (C + B).

The question is whether that split is right. Drug B is not really a new treatment for this patient — they had it before. The proposed change: when a drug from a previous line comes back, do **not** start a new line. Let it join the current line instead.

One exception stays either way: if a **genuinely new** drug starts on the same day the old drug returns, the line still splits — the new drug earns the split on its own.

In our data, 1,408 patients have a line break caused only by a returning previous-line drug. Under the proposed rule those breaks would go away. Another 39 patients have a returning drug plus a genuinely new drug on the same day — those breaks stay. Most returns happen soon: 1,114 of the 1,408 are within 6 months of the drug's last cover; only 92 are more than a year later.

